; the target bindu for the end-effector in world-space (sthira-apeksha)
; a single point in n-dimensional space — not split into x, y, z components
; 
; dimensionality matches the arm's workspace:
; 2-DOF planar arm: target is [x, y] in aayaama-dvaya
; 3-DOF spatial arm: target is [x, y, z] in aayaama-traya
; n-DOF arm:         target is a bindu in the appropriate aayaama
; 
; the target IS a bindu — zero-extent pure position in akasham
; it lives in sthira-apeksha (world frame) — it does not move when joints move
; the IK problem: find joint-angles such that fk(joint-angles) = target-position
; 
; scene extraction:
; "move end effector to (0.6, 0.3, 0.4)" → target-position = [0.6, 0.3, 0.4]
; "reach position x=3, y=4" → target-position = [3, 4]
; the dimensionality is inferred from the number of coordinates given
; and matched against the arm's aayaama (workspace dimension)
; a bindu in world-space — sthira-apeksha (does not move with joints)
; n-dimensional — aayaama determined by workspace
; the IK problem connects this to joint-angles via forward kinematics
; metre in each dimension

(kosha target-position
  (swarupa subanta bindu)
  (sthita domain-robotics world-space sthira-apeksha)
  (yukta kinematic-chain aayaama)
  (drishthanta aayaama-dvaya aayaama-traya)
  (ahara ik)
  (pratipaksha fk-phala)
  (matra metre))
